Three parts of GenOffice are ported into Polaris.

`dashboard/packages/core/src/sheets/` is its spreadsheet domain
(`apps/sheets/src/domain`, plus the pivot definition types from
`apps/sheets/src/gateway/xlsx-pivot.ts`): the pivot-table engine, the chart
model and recommender, flash fill, range sorting, formula reference shifting,
the A1 address reader and the in-memory workbook the rest are written against.

Why it is here rather than written again: Polaris' spreadsheet is built on
Univer, whose open edition ships the grid, the formulas and the formatting and
deliberately does not ship pivot tables, charts or flash fill - those are what
its makers sell. GenOffice had written exactly those, as pure TypeScript with
no framework in it, under a licence that permits this.

`dashboard/packages/pptx/` is `packages/pptx-engine`: the OOXML presentation
model, its parser and its generator - layouts, masters, themes, charts, shapes,
animations, comments, sections.

`dashboard/packages/docx/` is `packages/docx-engine`: the OOXML document model,
its parser, its fragment generators and a paragraph-level patching save -
styles, numbering, sections, fields, tables, drawings, maths.

`dashboard/packages/pptx-render/` is `packages/pptx-render`: drawing a parsed
presentation - text layout, shape geometry, fills, preset geometries.

`dashboard/packages/font-metrics/` is `packages/font-metrics`: reading the
tables a layout needs to measure text, without a font renderer.

`dashboard/packages/file-parse/` is `packages/file-parse`: taking the words out
of a document whatever it is - the Office formats, PDF, and the binary ones from
before them.

`dashboard/packages/pdf2docx/` is `packages/pdf2docx`: turning a PDF into an
editable Word document - text runs, columns, tables, images.

Together these are why an exported `.docx` or `.pptx` from Polaris is a real
Office package rather than a zip with the minimum set of parts in it, and why
reading one is something this repository can do at all. Every ported package is
held to the TypeScript strictness it was written under rather than Polaris' own,
which is stated in its `tsconfig.json`; everything Polaris writes against them is
still checked strictly at the call site.

`dashboard/packages/office-layout/` is the framework-free geometry out of its
three editors (`apps/docs`, `apps/slides`, `apps/pdf`): word counting in both
kinds of script, print ranges, footnote marks, background removal, slide ruler
ticks and snapping, CJK script classification, cover cropping, and a PDF's text
blocks, columns, spreads and search index. Only the files that stand on their
own came across - the pagination engine, for one, reaches the editor's own DOM
and would have meant dragging half an application over to satisfy it.

Also ported, each in its own package: `genoffice-i18n` (language detection and
message lookup), `project-store` (a project's files and their recent history),
`genoffice-agent` (its agent loop - turns, tools, results), `genoffice-ai` (its
model providers and request shapes), `genoffice-search` (search and image
generation through the Genspark CLI), `genoffice-ui` (its ribbon, colour picker,
shape gallery and Markdown view), and `office-html` (the half of its shell
helpers that is URL and remote-image safety rather than shell).

Polaris has its own of several of those already and uses its own. They are here
because the goal asked for the repository, and a package nobody imports still
costs nothing to keep; `@polaris/genoffice-ui` in particular is kept strictly
apart from `@polaris/ui`, which is Polaris' own design system.

`dashboard/packages/genoffice-docs/`, `genoffice-slides/`, `genoffice-pdf/` and
`genoffice-markdown/` are the four editor applications - their `renderer` half,
which is React and Konva, together with the `shared` IPC contract, which is
types only and describes the bridge each one expects.

They compile and they are not wired to a route. The bridge they talk to
(`window.slidesApi`, `window.docsApi` and so on) is an Electron channel injected
by a preload script; Polaris' equivalent is server actions, and writing that
adapter is a separate piece of work from carrying the code across. Polaris' own
Office apps are unaffected and remain what `/office` serves: these are here as
the repository asked, not as a replacement, and they have no real-time
collaboration in them - which is the first thing Polaris' own editors were built
to do.

What could NOT be taken, and why:

- **The Electron main and preload processes** of all four applications, and six
  of `electron-utils`' twelve modules - a menu bar, a native file dialog, a print
  window. They import `electron`. There is no version of them that runs in a
  browser.
- **The pagination engine, the line-metrics table and the shape
  adjust-handles**, as standalone geometry. They were tried for
  `office-layout`, and each reaches its own editor's DOM - so they came across
  inside their editor's package instead, where that DOM exists.

Everything else in that repository is here.
